# ffi/logs/rust/stage3-decode-utf8-policy.log
# W3: the DECODE side's UTF-8 policy, priced. ABI v1 open decision 3, THIRD framing.
# 2026-09-18
#
# CONFIGURATION (R7)
#   machine        4 vCPU Intel Xeon @ 2.80GHz, 15 GB, Ubuntu 24.04.4, Linux 6.18.44 x86_64,
#                  container, NOT an isolated host. No CPU pinning, no governor control.
#   rust           rustc/cargo 1.94.1, profile release (opt-level 3, lto off)
#   MSRV declared  1.88. NOT VERIFIED: no 1.88 toolchain exists in this container.
#   incumbent      prost 0.14.4
#   LINKAGE        cdylib (libak_core.so), dynamic linker. Not a static link.
#   guard          on
#   validators     core::str::from_utf8 (scalar, std) and simdutf8 0.1 (basic). simdutf8
#                  dispatches on runtime CPU features (AVX2 here); on a machine without
#                  them it falls back, so this row is machine-dependent in a way the
#                  scalar row is not.
#   class          M1 (P1.2) and M2 (P2.2) are both REAL messages (design/SHAPES.md).
#                  No control payloads here.
#   content sets   ascii / latin1 (U+00A0-U+00FF, 2 bytes each) / wide (above U+00FF,
#                  3 bytes each). IN RUST these price VALIDATION AND WIDTH, not a
#                  transcoder: a Rust String is UTF-8 already, so there is no narrowing
#                  and no conversion. A managed slice must not read these rows across.
#
# WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY THIS LOG EXISTS
#   The earlier decision-3 work priced UTF-8 validation on ENCODE. The third framing
#   removes it from there entirely: a `string` field on the wire is a length prefix and a
#   byte copy, the receiving parser cannot trust the bytes whatever the sender did, and
#   proto3 puts the obligation on parsers. So the question moved sides, and this log
#   answers the moved question: what does validate-and-REJECT cost on decode, against the
#   lossy decode this slice shipped before.
#
#   Confirmed in this tree, not inferred: `ak_tc_bytes()` and `ak_tc_utf8_trusted()`
#   return the SAME function pointer (`tc_utf8_trusted`, crates/ak-core/src/lib.rs:342-348).
#   The collapse the design predicts has already happened in the implementation.
#
#   The encode rows elsewhere in this slice already quote the passthrough:
#   `Ctx::new()` builds `Tcs::trusted()` (crates/harness/src/arms.rs:125), so every
#   `core-ffi-rust` encode figure in stage2-four-arms-M1.log, stage3-*.log and
#   stage3-content-sets.log is ALREADY the passthrough column. The `core-ffi (valid utf8)`
#   and `core-ffi (valid, simd)` rows in stage3-content-sets.log are the retired
#   measurement of the previous framing and must not be quoted as a default.
#
# WHAT WAS BUILT FOR THIS LOG
#   crates/ak-rt/src/strings.rs: `decode_str`, one place, three BUILD-TIME policies.
#     default              lossy   String::from_utf8_lossy, U+FFFD, cannot fail
#     --features dec-reject         reject  core::str::from_utf8      -> AK_ERR_TRANSCODE
#     --features dec-reject-simd    reject  simdutf8::basic           -> AK_ERR_TRANSCODE
#   The generator was swept, not patched at a site: every string materialisation in
#   crates/facade/src/generated/core_native.rs (37 sites) and every `s_of` in
#   crates/harness/src/generated/binding.rs (38 sites) goes through it. `from_utf8_lossy`
#   no longer appears in either generated file.
#
#   The error code is AK_ERR_TRANSCODE (-6), because that is the code the design text
#   names for this case. AK_ERR_MALFORMED (-2, "invalid wire") is the other defensible
#   reading -- proto3 makes invalid UTF-8 a PARSE error and the rejecting path has no
#   transcoder on it. That choice is not a slice's to settle; it is raised, not taken.
#
# MEASUREMENT HAZARDS THIS TABLE IS EXPOSED TO (R9)
#   1. THE POLICY IS A BUILD-TIME CHOICE, SO THE THREE COLUMNS ARE THREE PROCESSES.
#      R4 does not hold across them. The in-process control is the `prost` row: prost
#      validates UTF-8 on decode under every one of these builds and the feature flag
#      cannot reach it. `/ prost` is therefore the only column that travels between the
#      three policy sections, and it is also the direct answer to "what does a rejecting
#      decode do to the ratio against the incumbent".
#   2. A runtime-dispatched policy was deliberately NOT built: a branch per string would
#      be a cost of its own inside the measurement it exists to make, and it would put
#      that cost into the shipped decode path as well.
#   3. Container, shared vCPU, no pinning. Run-to-run spread is reported per row
#      (`spread %` = (max-min)/median over 11 rounds) and two runs per policy are printed
#      so a reader can see the spread rather than take a median on trust.
#   4. Decode here materialises a full owned facade value in every arm, so a string cost
#      is a validation cost ON TOP OF an allocation and a copy that all three policies
#      pay equally. A zero-copy decode would show the validation as a larger fraction.
#
# THE CASE THAT MUST FAIL (D13)
#   Section 2 of each run plants a 10-byte ASCII marker in ResultRaw.name, finds it in the
#   encoded bytes (asserting it occurs exactly once), and overwrites its first byte with
#   0xFF -- never a legal UTF-8 lead byte. The length prefix is unchanged, so the WIRE is
#   still well formed and nothing but the UTF-8 policy can notice. prost rejects it in
#   every build, which is what makes prost usable as the reference for "what a conformant
#   parser does". The sticky error slot is then proved cleared: a good decode after a
#   rejected one must succeed, or every timing below would be measuring a poisoned context.
#
# A HAZARD FOUND WHILE TAKING THESE NUMBERS, AND WHAT WAS DONE ABOUT IT
#   The first version of the driver built and ran each policy in turn, so the lossy build
#   was always the first process. Its `core-native` P1.2 ascii row came out at 0.778 of
#   prost in one invocation and 0.88 in the next, from the SAME source, with the prost
#   control unmoved. A rebuilt binary moves by more than the effect on some rows, and with
#   a fixed order that movement is indistinguishable from a policy effect.
#   Two things were changed and both are in this log:
#     - the three binaries are built FIRST and then run ROUND ROBIN with a rotating order
#       (rounds 1-3 below), so no policy is always first and the prost control can be read
#       across the three builds to see whether the machine moved;
#     - section 4 was added, which prices the three policies AGAINST EACH OTHER IN ONE
#       PROCESS over the string corpus, so the policy question has an answer that does not
#       depend on three builds agreeing about anything.
#   Section 4 and section 3 agree in direction and magnitude, which is why the numbers are
#   reported rather than a third attempt made.
#
# WHAT SECTION 4 IS AND IS NOT
#   It is the string materialisation alone -- validate, then allocate and copy -- over
#   EVERY `string` field of the decoded payload, harvested by reflection over the
#   descriptor so that "every" is a fact about the descriptor and not a hand-written list.
#   It is NOT a decode: it has no wire reading, no container construction and no boundary.
#   Section 3 is what the policy does to a decode; section 4 is what the policy costs.
#
# WHAT THIS LOG SAYS, BEFORE THE NUMBERS
#   1. VALIDATE-AND-REJECT IS NOT A COST ON DECODE. On the string path alone (section 4,
#      one process) the scalar rejecting policy is 0.54 to 0.75 of today's lossy path on
#      ascii, 0.83 to 0.97 on latin1 and 0.94 to 1.10 on wide. The reason is that
#      `from_utf8_lossy` ALREADY VALIDATES -- it substitutes instead of failing -- and its
#      chunked recovery path is slower than `from_utf8`. Rejecting buys correctness and
#      costs, at worst, a tenth of the string path on the widest content.
#   2. WITH `simdutf8::basic` IT IS CHEAPER THAN TODAY'S DECODE ON EVERY CONTENT SET:
#      0.53 to 0.71 of lossy on ascii, 0.45 to 0.68 on latin1, 0.36 to 0.60 on wide.
#   3. THIS EXACTLY INVERTS THE ENCODE RESULT. The same scalar validator, on encode, turned
#      a 0.72-0.81 win against prost into a 2.0-2.6 loss (stage3-content-sets.log), because
#      there it was pure ADDED work on top of a memcpy. On decode it replaces work that was
#      already being done. "Expensive checking where it is redundant, silent substitution
#      where proto3 says a parser must validate" now has a price on both sides.
#   4. A REJECTING DECODE DOES NOT COST THE COMPARISON AGAINST PROST -- IT IMPROVES IT.
#      prost rejects malformed UTF-8 (its message is in section 2 of every run below), so
#      moving to reject makes the two sides do the same thing, and the ratio moves in our
#      favour or not at all: on P1.2 ascii from 0.87-0.91 to 0.73-0.79, on P1.2 wide from
#      0.78-0.80 to 0.49-0.51 with the SIMD validator. The previous decode column was
#      PESSIMISTIC, not flattering: it was paying for a slower validator to get a weaker
#      guarantee.
#   5. THE MAIN DECODE TABLES IN STATE.md WERE TAKEN WITH THE LOSSY POLICY and are not
#      rewritten here. If the design adopts a rejecting decode, the ASCII decode column
#      improves by roughly 0.08-0.12 on M1 and 0.03-0.08 on M2, in this slice's favour.
#
