#
#   THE CONTROL ROW IS NOT PERFECTLY FLAT. prost moves by up to 7 percent between the
#   three builds on the two `wide` rows (P1.2 840-845 against 778-785; P2.2 2655-2693
#   against 2479-2610) although the flag cannot reach it. That is the between-build drift
#   the round robin exposes rather than hides, and it is why the `wide` rows are quoted as
#   ranges and why section 4 exists. Effects smaller than about 5 points of ratio on those
#   two rows are not distinguishable from it.
#
# SECTION 4, the policy alone, ONE process, min-max over all nine runs.
#
# payload set     strings    bytes  B/str   scalar/lossy    simd/lossy
# P1.2    ascii      5000   151989   30.4    0.535-0.601   0.534-0.593
# P1.2    latin1     5000   303978   60.8    0.828-0.955   0.447-0.538
# P1.2    wide       5000   455967   91.2    0.937-1.105   0.362-0.414
# P2.2    ascii     17167   412032   24.0    0.661-0.746   0.641-0.712
# P2.2    latin1    17167   816064   47.5    0.849-0.973   0.590-0.680
# P2.2    wide      17167  1220096   71.1    0.962-1.049   0.503-0.597
#
#   The string path is a large share of a decode here: on P1.2 ascii the corpus costs
#   about 190 us against a whole core-native decode of about 410 us in the same build, so
#   section 3's movements are the size section 4 predicts. The two tables were taken by
#   different means and agree in direction and in magnitude.
#
# CORRECTNESS, EVERY RUN
#   - byte identity against the validated manifest under all three policy builds
#     (conformance, above), so no policy changes an encode or a valid decode;
#   - value identity of both core arms against the facade value, on all three content
#     sets, in every run;
#   - the malformed case: prost rejects it in every build; the core arms substitute under
#     the lossy build and return -6 (AK_ERR_TRANSCODE) through `ak_fail` under both
#     rejecting builds; and a good decode after a rejected one succeeds, which is the
#     sticky slot being cleared and not a poisoned context.
#
# WHAT THIS LOG DOES NOT MEASURE
#   - The ENCODE side is untouched here. The encode rows elsewhere already quote the
#     passthrough; no new encode measurement was taken and none was needed.
#   - The opt-in diagnostic encode mode was NOT built, by instruction.
#   - Only P1.2 and P2.2. The other payloads' decode figures in the other logs remain
#     lossy-policy figures.
#   - One machine, AVX2 present. The `simdutf8` rows in particular are one machine; on a
#     host without AVX2 `simdutf8::basic` falls back and this table's second conclusion
#     does not automatically hold.
#   - Nothing here prices what a REJECT does to a caller. A conformant parser rejects the
#     whole message, so a single bad string loses a batch of a thousand results. That is a
#     behavioural cost this slice cannot put a number on.
